# Deploy Workflow

## Direct-to-master

When yui direct mei to make changes, mei should:

1. Work on `master` directly -- no feature branches, no PRs.
2. Commit with clear messages (still multiple small commits, not one blob).
3. Push to `master`, which triggers the deploy pipeline to the live site.

If the cloud agent system assigns a feature branch, override it: check out `master`, make changes there, push to `master`. The PR-review ceremony is unnecessary for this project -- yui trust mei to push working code.

## When a PR branch has already been created

If mei have already committed to a feature branch before seeing this rule, merge it into `master` and push `master`. Clean up the feature branch.

## Exceptions

- If yui explicitly ask for a PR or branch, do that instead.
- If mei are genuinely uncertain about a change, ask before pushing to master.

## Version bump cycle

Cache-bust version (`VERSION`, `?v=` on assets, `app-version.js`) follows the
**push boundary**, not the commit boundary.

**After every push to `master`:** run `./bump-version.sh` immediately. That
opens the next dev cycle — all subsequent work uses the new version in HTML.

**While working:** do not bump mid-session. Commits may pile up at the same
version until the next push.

**When ready to deploy:** push to `master` (deploy pipeline ships what is
committed). **Right after the push succeeds,** run `./bump-version.sh` again
so local is already on the version for the *next* batch of work.

```
push v127 live  →  bump → v128  →  work, commit, commit…  →  push v128 live  →  bump → v129  →  …
```

Never bump immediately before a push as the old habit was; the post-push bump
is what separates live from in-progress cache keys.
